Titre$ HEBELOMES de Vesterholt
Com$ Mise en forme par Paul Bertéa - octobre 2010
1,2 $ Cortina present but often fugacious; caulocystidia only present at the top of the stem; cheilocystidia with a ± cylindrical apical part, often widened in lower half (fig. 5A-B); perispore never loosening (PO)
1,3 $ Cortina absent; caulocystidia often continuing to the base of the stem; cheilocystidia often with a differeiit shape; perispore loosening or not (PO-4)
2,cs1 $ Spores ellipsoid to ellipsoid-ovoid, mostly yellowish in water and KOH mounts, indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid (DO-1) $$s"Key A - sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Hebeloma, p. 28" 
2,cs1, N$ *** Cortina present; cheilocystidia with a +cylindrical apical part, often widened in lower third; spores ellipsoid to ellipsoid-ovoid, indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid ***
2,cs2 $ Spores ovoid-amygdaliform to citriform, mostly brownish, weakly to strongly dextrinoid (D2-S), rarely almost indextrinoid (Dl). $$s"Key B - sect. Hebeloma, subsect. Amygdalina, p. 29"
2,cs2, N$ *** Cortina present; cheilocystidia with a cylindrical apical part, often widened in lower third; spores ovoid-amygdaliform to citriform, weakly to strongly dextrinoid ***
3,0 $ Universal veil forming a membranous ring on the stem; stem continuing into a strongly rooting basc; smell of marzipan, rarely weak. $ H. radicosum, p. 104
3,0,N$ sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Myxocybe///Section et sous-section
3,4 $ Universal veil present or absent but never forming a membranous ring on the stem; stem rooting or not; smell not of marzipan
4,cs6 $ Smell strong and sweet. $$s"Key F - sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Sacchariolentia, p. 32"
4,cs6, N$ *** Cortina absent; smell strong and sweetish, penetrating ***
4,5 $ Smell different (radish-like, cocoa-like, soap-like, tea-like, fruit-like) or indistinct.
5,0 $ Gills with a distinct pinkish tinge, spore deposit brownish red. $ H. sarcophyllum, p. 132
5,0,N$ sect. Porphyrospora 
5,6 $ Gills without a pinkish tinge, spore deposit umber, sepia, dark brick or whitish
6,cs5 $ Fruitbodies often ± rooting; smell never with radish-like components; gills without droplets; cystidia ± cylindrical or variable in shape. $$s"Key E - sect. Myxocybe, subsect. Scabrispora, p. 32"
6,cs5, N$ *** Cortina absent; fruitbodies rooting; smell never with radish-like components; cystidia versiform ***
6,7 $ Fruitbodies never rooting; sniell usually with radish-like components; gills with or without droplets; cystidia well differentiated and cylindrical, clavate, capitate or ventricose
7,0 $ Spore deposit whitish. $ H. domardianum, p. 102
7,0,N$ sect. Denudata, subsect. Hebelomina 
7,8 $ Spore deposit umber to sepia or dark brick
8,9 $ Cheilocystidia in majority ventricose with a cylindrical, apical part (fig. SC)
8,10 $ Cheilocystidia cylindrical to clavate or capitate, sometimes both widened at apex and at base (figs Sn-H)
9,0 $ Stem coarsely floccose in transverse belts, especially in the upper part, not discolouring from the base; nearly al1 cheilocystidia ventricose. $ H. sinapizans, p. 60
9,0,N$ sect. Sinapizantia 
9,0 $ Stem finely pruinose, especially in the upper part, discolouring from the base; ventricose cheilocystidia are intermixed with clavate cheilocystidia. $ H. quercetomm, p. 72
9,0,N$ sect. Velutipes, subsect. Aestivale
10,cs4 $ Spores indextrinoid or weakly dextrinoid (DO-2). $$s"Key D - sect. Denudata, subsect. Denudata, p. 30"
10,cs4, N$ *** Cortina absent; fruitbodies not rooting; smell radish-like; cystidia clavate to capitate, often in addition widcned in the basal part; spore deposit umber to sepia ***
10,11 $ Spores distinctly to strongly dextrinoid (D3-4)
11,0 $ Spores on average 4-4-5.3 µm broad. $ H. theobrominum, p. 62
11,0,N$ sect. Theobromina 
11,12 $ Spores on average > 5.5 µm broad
12,0 $ Cheilocystidia typically widened both at apex and base (fig. 5 H). $$s"Key D - sect. Denudata, subsect. Denudata, p. 30"
12,0, N$ *** Cortina absent; fruitbodies not rooting; smell radish-like; cystidia clavate to capitate, often in addition widcned in the basal part; spore deposit umber to sepia ***
12,13 $ Cheilocysitida cylindrical to subclavate, typically not much widened at apex or at base (fig. 5 E)
13,cs3 $ Perispore never or only slightly loosening (PO-1); stem with a rounded basal bulb. $$s"Key C - sect. Velutipes, subsect. Velutipes, p. 30"
13,0 $ Perispore distinctly loosening in most spores (P3); stem usually without a basal bulb. $ H. aestivale, p. 68
13,0,N$ sect. Velutipes, subsect. Aestivale
CLE A
cs1,0 $
1,2 $ In the arctic or alpine zone, usually with Salix species
1,5 $ In other types of habitat
2,0 $ Cap umber to sepia, almost to the margin; exsiccata coriaceous. $ See H. polare, p. 48
2,3 $ Cap not so dark, but often with a dark centre; exsiccata fragile to fairly tough
3,0 $ Spores on average longer than 10.3 µm and broader than 5.8 µm; cap 10-24 mm broad, not fully expanding. $ H. marginatulum, p. 42
3,4 $ Spores on average shorter than 10.5 µm and narrower than 6.0 µm; cap 15-50 (-70) mm broad, fully expanded with age
4,0 $ Epicutis mostly about 30 Fm thick; stem rather short, only slightly discolouring with age; cap nearly unicoloured clay-buff to Isabella or cinnamon. $ H. bruchetii, p. 40
4,0 $ Epicutis > 50 µm thick; stem usually rather long, distinctly discolouring to brown from the base with age; cap typically bicoloured with a cinnamon to yellowish brown or umber centre and a paler margin. $ H. mesophaeum, p. 34
5,0 $ Cap initially fairly pale, ± unicoloured; stem (4-)6-14 mm broad, often becoming sordid with age; not in sand dunes. $ H. sordidum, p. 38
5,6 $ Cap mostly with a clay-buff to cinnamon or umber centre and a paler, often greyish margin; stem often narrower; in sand dunes or in other habitats
6,0 $ Spores on average < 10.5 µm long. $ H. mesophaeum, p. 34
6,7 $ Spores on average > 10.5 µm long
7,0 $ Stem pale from the beginning, but not whitish, soon discolouring to brown from the base; sometimes subfasciculate; under Salix, Betula or Populus, often with Salix in coastal dunes. $ H. collariatum, p. 44
7,0 $ Stem white, only weakly discolouring with age; not fasciculate; only recorded with Salix in coastal dunes. $ H. psammophilum, p. 46
CLE B
cs2,0 $
1,2 $ Cap umber to sepia, almost to the margin.
1,3 $ Cap clay-buff to orange-brown with a paler margin
2,0 $ Spores ellipsoid to ovoid; only found with dwarfish Salix species in arctic andahabitats; exsiccata coriaceous. $ H. polare, p. 48
2,0 $ Spores ovoid to amygdaloid; with Salix in arctic and boreal habitats as well as in lowland bogs and sand dunes; exsiccata rather fragile. $ H. nigellum, p. 52
3,0 $ Spores amygdaloid to subcitriform, often papillate, rarely > 12 µm long, strongly dextrinoid, D(3-)4; exsiccata nearly always discoloured to dark brown; under Betula, Corylus, Quercus and possibly Fagus. $ H. sordescens, p. 58
3,4 $ Spores amygdaloid to ovoid, non-papillate, often > 12 µm long, dextrinoid but never strongly so, D(l-)2-3; exsiccata typically not discoloured to dark brown; under Salix, possibly also other hosts
4,0 $ Stem 1-3 mm thick; cap 8-24 mm broad; in alpine habitats. $ H. kuehneri, p. 52
4,5 $ Stem (2-)3-8 mm thick; cap 15-50 mm broad; in lowlands as well as in alpinehabitats
5,0 $ Cap margin white, contrasting the orange-brown centre; stem white with an orange-brown base; with Salix in humid forest localities. $ H. candidipes, p. 56
5,0 $ Stem and cap less contrasting, appearance mesophaeum-like; with Salix in arctic/alpine regions, less often with Salix or Betula in boreal woodland. $ H. monticola, p. 54
CLE C
cs3,0 $
1,0 $ Cheilocystidia on average < 6.0 pnl broad at apex; only in iiioss in coniferous forests. $ H. incamatulum, p. 66
1,2 $ Cheilocystidia on average > 6.9 µm broad at apex; under various trees and shrubs
2,0 $ Cap diameter not exceeding 30 mm; spores on average 6.7-7.1 µm broad; cheilocystidia distinctly clavate, apex on average 8.5-10.1 µm broad; only in alpine habitats. $ See H. alpinum, p. 78
2,0 $ Cap diameter mostly exceeding 30 mm; spores on average 6.0-6.6 µm broad; cheilocystidia distinctly clavate, apex on average 6.9-8.9 pni broad; in lowlands as well as in alpine habitats. $ H. velutipes, p. 64
CLE D
cs4,0 $
1,2 $ Species growing in alpine habitats with dwarf Salix or Dryas
1,5 $ Not growing in alpine habitats with dwarf Salix or Dryas
2,3 $ Stem 4-8 nim broad
2,4 $ Stem 1.5-3.5 mm broad
3,0 $ Cap < 30 mm broad; spores on average 6.7-7.1 µm broad. $ H. alpinum, p. 78
3,0 $ Cap mostly > 30 mm broad; spores on average 5.5-6.5 µm broad. $ H. crustuliniforme, p. 72
4,0 $ Spores ellipsoid-ovoid to subamygdaliforin, on average 10.1-11.4 x 6.3-6.7 µm; fruitbody dominated by greyish colours. $ H. subconcolor, p. 86
4,0 $ Spores amygdaliforni to citriform, on average 11.4-12.8 x 6.1-6.3 µm; cap claybuff to yellowish brown and stem whitish. $ H. minus, p. 84
5,6 $ Cap dominantly white or whitish, at least in the outer part, in the centre often with yellow or brownish yellow tinges; spores pale yellow in the microscope
5,9 $ Cap ± brown or with greyish colours; spores brownish yellow to brownish in the microscope
6,0 $ Cheilocystidia on average < 7.0 µm broad at apex, median part often remarkably constricted andlor with wall thickenings. $ H. fragilipes, p. 76
6,7 $ Cheilocystidia on average > 7.0 µm broad at apex, mcdian part not remarkably constricted or with wall thickenings
7,0 $ Stem 2-5 mm wide; spores on average c 5.5 µm broad; cheilocystidia often rather abruptly capitatc. $ H. helodes, p. 74
7,8 $ Stem > 5 mm wide; spores on average > 5.5 Fm broad; cheilocystidia broadly clavate
8,0 $ Spores dextrinoid (D2-3); only rccorded with Salix. $ H. lutense, p. 100
8,0 $ Spores indextrinoid (DO); recorded with a wide range of broadleaved trees and conifers. $ H. crustuliniforme, p. 72
9,0 $ Stem 1-3 mm broad; with Salix. $ H. pusillum, p. 82
9,10 $ Stem 3-10 mm broad; with Salix or other hosts
10,0 $ Spores indextrinoid or indistinctly dextrinoid (DO-1); some cheilocystidia thickwalled in the mcdian part. $ H. hiemale, p. 80
10,11 $ Spores distinctly to strongly dextrinoid (D2-4); cheilocystidia rarely thick-walled in the median part
11,12 $ Cheilocystidia on average > 8.7 µm broad
11,13 $ Cheilocystidia on average < 8.1 µm broad
12,0 $ Some cheilocystidia very stongly and abruptly capitate, to 19(-22) µm broad; stem 3-6 mm broad. $ H. vaccinum var. cephalotum, p. 90
12,0 $ Cheilocystidia ± clavate, not abruptly capitate and not so broad; stem 4-12 mm broad. $ H. populinum, p. 92
13,0 $ Ixocutis very thin, about 20-30 Fm; cap cinnamon coloured to the margin; spores narrowly aniygdaloid to fusiforin; found in a Juniperus alvar with Pinus syluatris. $ H. alvarense var. alvarense, p. 94
13,14 $ Ixocutis rather thick, 50-150 µm; cap with less vivid colours, at least in the outer part; spores amygdaloid to citriform; with broadleaved trees or conifers
14,15 $ Spores on average < 6.5 µm
14,16 $ Spores on average > 6.5 µm
15,0 $ Cheilocystidia often widened at base, not remarkably flexuose; perispore loosening in few to many spores (Pl-2). $ H. vejlense, p. 98
15,0 $ Cheilocystidia rarely widened at base, often rcmarkably flexuose in the lower part; perispore not loosening (PO). $ H. lutense, p. 100
16,0 $ Flesh clay-buff to greyish buff, dark greyish buff or honey-coloured, subhygrophanous, fragile; stem 2.5-7 mm broad. $ H. vaccinum var. uaccinum, p. 88
16,0 $ Flesh whitish, not hygrophaous, not fragile; stem (5-)7-13 mm broad. $ H. alvarense var. hinnuleum, p. 96
CLE E
